course: biology
lesson: 21
title: Elephants: the trunk and everything it does
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An elephant has one trunk, two ears, four legs, two eyes and one heart, but the trunk is the part that does almost everything: it is a nose, a hand, a hose, a snorkel and a trumpet all at once.
explain: The trunk is the elephant's nose and top lip grown long and joined together, with two nostrils running all the way down it and no bones at all, only tens of thousands of little muscle bundles.
explain: Picture the zoo: the elephant reaches down, pinches one peanut with the tip of its trunk, curls it up and pops it into its mouth, because a short neck and a huge head mean the mouth can't reach the ground.
explain: Then it sucks up about 8 litres of water, squirts it into its mouth like a hose, and afterwards throws dust over its back as sunscreen and bug spray.
explain: Cartoons show elephants drinking through the trunk like a straw and blowing water out like a fireman; the second part is right, but the water goes up the trunk, stops, and is blown into the mouth.
fact: The trunk is the elephant's nose and upper lip joined together and grown very long.
fact: A trunk has no bones but tens of thousands of muscle bundles, while your whole body has about 600 muscles.
fact: An African elephant has two finger-like tips at the end of its trunk, and an Asian elephant has one, so it often curls its trunk round things to pick them up.
fact: An adult elephant can hold about 8 litres of water in its trunk and then blows it into its mouth; it does not drink through it like a straw, and it drinks well over 100 litres a day.
fact: Elephants throw dust and mud over their backs, which protects their sensitive skin from sunburn and biting insects, and the deep wrinkles of their skin hold water and mud that cool them.
fact: Elephants can swim and use the trunk as a snorkel, keeping only its tip above the water.
fact: Elephants have a superb sense of smell and more smell genes than dogs, and lift the trunk to catch scents on the wind.
fact: Elephants greet and comfort each other by touching faces and twisting their trunks together.
fact: An elephant eats around 150 kilograms of plants a day and spends up to 16 to 18 hours a day eating, because plants give little goodness in each mouthful.
fact: A newborn calf can't control its trunk yet; it practises for months, sometimes sucking it like a thumb, and until then it kneels to drink with its mouth.
fact: Elephants may jump at small sudden things, but the story that they are specially afraid of mice is a myth.
fact: Elephants sometimes use tools, such as a stick to scratch an itch or a leafy branch to swat flies.
fact: The trumpet call is air blasted out through the trunk; elephants also make deep rumbles other elephants feel through their feet.
fact: The tip of the trunk is as sensitive as our fingertips, so elephants tap and feel things to test them, such as whether ground is safe.
fact: The ancestors of elephants, around 55 million years ago, were pig-sized animals with no trunk.
exercise: What two parts of the face joined to make the trunk? | The nose and the upper lip | The nose and the lower jaw | The upper lip and the tongue | The neck and the nose
exercise: How many bones are there in an elephant's trunk? | None | One long bone | About 600 small bones | Exactly 40 bones
exercise: How many finger-like tips does an African elephant have at the end of its trunk? | Two | One | Three | Four
exercise: How does an elephant at a waterhole drink? | It sucks water into its trunk, then blows it into its mouth | It drinks through the trunk like a straw into its tummy | It kneels and laps with its tongue like a dog | It soaks water in through its feet
exercise: About how much water can an adult elephant hold in its trunk at once? | About 8 litres | About half a litre | About 80 litres | About 800 litres
exercise: Why does an elephant spray dust over its back on a hot afternoon? | It protects its skin from sun and insects | It is trying to look grey like the others | It is cleaning the dust off its trunk | It wants to make itself heavier
exercise: How can an elephant breathe while it swims across a deep river? | It holds the tip of its trunk up like a snorkel | It breathes through little gills hidden behind its big ears | It holds its breath for a whole day | It cannot swim and walks on the bottom
exercise: Why does an elephant lift its trunk high in the air? | To sniff smells carried on the wind | To look further over the tall grass | To show it is about to sit down | To cool its trunk in the breeze
exercise: What does it mean when two elephants twist their trunks together? | They are greeting each other like a handshake | They are starting a fight | They are stuck and can't let go | They are measuring to see whose trunk is the longer one
exercise: About how much plant food does an adult elephant eat in a day? | About 150 kilograms | About 1.5 kilograms | About 15 kilograms | About 1,500 kilograms
exercise: Why does a baby elephant trip over its own trunk? | It hasn't learned to control all those muscles yet | Its trunk is longer than its mother's | Its trunk has no muscles at all in it until it is ten years old | It is blind for its first year
exercise: An elephant holds a stick in its trunk to scratch its back. What is the stick? | A tool | A toy it will eat later | A spare tusk | A piece of its own trunk
exercise: How does an elephant make its loud trumpet call? | It blasts air out through its trunk | It claps its ears together | It stamps its feet on hollow ground | It whistles through its tusks
exercise: Why does an elephant tap a bridge with its trunk before walking on? | The trunk tip feels things finely, so it tests whether it is safe | It is knocking to ask permission | It is marking the bridge with its smell | Elephants always tap three times for luck before they cross water
exercise: What did the ancestors of elephants look like about 55 million years ago? | Pig-sized animals with no trunk | Giant elephants with two trunks | Small elephants with wings | Whales with tusks
exercise: How many muscles does your whole body have, compared with the tens of thousands of bundles in a trunk? | About 600 | About 60 | About 60,000 | About 6
test: in the cartoon the elephant drinks thru its trunk like a straw lol. true? | No, it fills the trunk and squirts the water into its mouth | Yes, the trunk is a straw down to its tummy | Yes, but only baby elephants do it | No, elephants never use their trunk for water
test: Count them: how many trunks, ears and legs does one elephant have altogether? | 7: one trunk, two ears and four legs | 6: one trunk, one ear and four legs | 8: two trunks, two ears and four legs | 5: one trunk, two ears and two legs
test: What would happen if an elephant had a short nose like a horse but kept its short neck and heavy head? | It would struggle to reach food and water on the ground | It would eat more easily than before | It would grow a longer tail to help | Nothing, the trunk is only for smelling
test: My mate reckons the trunk is full of bones like a finger. Is he right? | No, it is all muscle with no bones at all | Yes, it has about 100 small bones | Yes, it has one bone like a long finger | No, it is hollow and filled only with air
test: An Asian elephant tries to pick up a peanut. How is it different from an African elephant? | It has one finger-like tip, so it often curls the trunk round things | It has three tips, so it is much quicker | It cannot pick up anything small at all | It uses its tusks instead of its trunk
test: Why might a zoo elephant splash mud over itself even though it isn't dirty? | Mud and dust protect its skin and the wrinkles hold water to cool it | It is hiding from the zookeeper | Its skin is so thick and tough that it feels nothing anyway | It needs the mud to make its tusks grow
test: oi why's the elephant just standing there sniffing the air like a weirdo | It's smelling for water or other elephants far away | It's checking if it's going to snow | It's trying to sneeze on the crowd | It can't see a thing, so it's smelling for the keeper with the food
test: Which is true about the elephant's sense of smell? | Elephants have more smell genes than dogs | Elephants can hardly smell anything | Elephants smell only with their mouths | Dogs can smell water further away than any elephant
test: A zookeeper hears a rumble too low for people to notice, and the other elephants turn. How did they get the message? | They felt the rumble through the ground with their feet | They read the first elephant's ears | They smelled the rumble in the air | They saw the keeper point at it
test: In a cartoon an elephant is scared of a tiny mouse and climbs a chair. What is the real story? | Elephants may startle at small things but are not specially afraid of mice | Elephants run from mice because mice climb up the trunk | Elephants are afraid only of grey mice | Every elephant faints when it sees a mouse
test: Why might a calf kneel down and drink with its mouth instead of its trunk? | It has not yet learned to drink with the trunk | Calves have no nostrils inside their trunk yet | Its mother forbids it to use the trunk | Calf trunks only work at night
test: Why does an elephant spend 16 to 18 hours a day eating? | It needs a huge amount of plant food, and plants are not very nourishing | It is bored and has nothing else to do | It eats only one tiny leaf at a time, picked up with its tusks | It stores the food in its trunk for winter
exam: Identify the anatomical structures from whose union the proboscis of the elephant is understood to have arisen. | The nose and the upper lip | The nose and the lower jaw | The upper lip and the tongue | The cheeks and the chin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which an adult elephant ingests water? | Water is drawn into the trunk and subsequently expelled into the mouth | Water passes directly through the trunk into the stomach | Water is absorbed through the skin of the feet | Water is lapped with the tongue in the manner of a dog
exam: Ascertain the number of bones contained within the trunk of an adult African elephant. | None whatsoever | Precisely one | Approximately six hundred | Approximately forty
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the application of dust by an elephant to its own dorsum. | It affords protection against solar radiation and biting insects | It serves to increase the animal's body mass | It is undertaken solely for purposes of camouflage against the keeper | It promotes the growth of the tusks
